# World of IUT

World of IUT is a C-based exploration game developed as part of the BUT Informatique program at IUT Saint-Dié-des-Vosges.

The project focuses on managing game locations, player movement and dynamic memory using appropriate data structures.

## Project Overview

The game represents a set of interconnected locations that the player can explore.

Each location contains information about its environment and the possible directions available to reach other locations.

The project also uses a Stack structure to manage locations and ensure an ordered release of dynamically allocated memory.

## Features

- Navigation between different game locations
- Representation of interconnected locations
- Display of location information
- Dynamic creation and deletion of locations
- Stack-based memory management
- Player movement between locations
- Initialization and shutdown of game resources

## Data Structures

### Location

The `Location` module represents the different places available in the game.

Main functions:

- `LocationNew` - Creates a new location in memory
- `LocationDelete` - Releases the memory allocated to a location
- `LocationPrint` - Displays information about a location
- `LocationInit` - Initializes the locations and their connections

### Stack

The `Stack` module provides a LIFO (Last In, First Out) structure used to manage locations during their destruction.

Main functions:

- `StackPush` - Adds a location to the stack
- `StackPop` - Removes the location at the top of the stack
- `StackHead` - Accesses the location at the top of the stack

## Player Movement

The player's position is associated with a `Location` through a pointer.

The `MobileMove` function allows the player to move dynamically between connected locations.

The game initialization and shutdown functions were also adapted to correctly initialize locations and release allocated resources.

## My Contribution

As part of the development team, my main contributions were:

- Implementation of the `Location` module
- Development of `LocationNew`, `LocationDelete` and `LocationPrint`
- Implementation of `LocationInit`
- Creation of connections between locations
- Implementation of the `Stack` data structure
- Development of the associated stack functions

